    isbefore: []  # 6d7c
    duplicate_of: []  # 6d7c
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 6d7c
    pr_ref:  # 6d7c
    description: "A CONSTRUCTOR declared under a data-transfer boundary: the family behind INV-nular's last adjudicated-and-unfixed row.\n\nTHE NAMED INSTANCE, adjudicated 2026-08-27 on INV-nular's own thread and re-verified PRESENT at tip 2026-09-08 (dev f4f278b736):\n\n    python.yaml  net_send  urllib.request.Request  (function, no notes)\n\n`Request` BUILDS a request object and sends nothing. It was filed under net_send beside `urlopen` and `urlretrieve`, which do send. The 08-27 entry adjudicated it and explicitly classed it as an ATTRIBUTION defect rather than a vacuous claim -- in jaeger's scripts/release/notes.py every `Request(...)` is consumed by `urlopen(req)` on the next line (L34/36, L39/41, L86/88, L116/119, L196/198), so the network boundary really is crossed; the sink is attributed one call too early. That is why this is NOT a drive-by row deletion: removing a net_send row is a RECALL change, and whether the flow survives depends on whether taint propagates through the constructed object into `urlopen`. Unmeasured.\n\nWHY IT SURVIVED EVERY SWEEP, which is the part worth keeping. The direction guard (test_inv_nular_false_sources.py::test_the_direction_sweep_is_otherwise_clean) is NAME-BASED: it examines a row only when the name carries a direction word from a curated vocabulary. Measured at tip: 598 of 3,405 loaded rows, 18%. \"Request\" is direction-SILENT, so the sweep never looked at it. This row is the worked example of the guard's stated limit, and the same limit already cost a whole family once -- the vocabulary held the verb `respond` and not the noun `response`, the sweep reported clean, and four Network.Wai rows survived to be found separately.\n\nTHE FAMILY, SIZED NOT ADJUDICATED (2026-09-08, ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/nular_rescope_09082026/ctor_scan.py). A base-catalogue scan for \"constructor-shaped name under a transfer boundary\" returns 181 candidates, 98 of which carry no `notes`. The stratum OVER-collects heavily and says so: 146 of the 181 are Go, where every exported identifier is capitalised, so `net.Conn.Write` matches a constructor pattern and is plainly correct. Reading the no-notes candidates gives three credible members:\n\n    python    net_send  urllib.request.Request     constructor; urlopen sends\n    go        net_send  net/http.NewRequest        same shape, same language pair\n    erlang    db_write  ets.new                    creates a table, stores nothing\n    elixir    db_write  ets.new                    (the same primitive)\n\nand one more in a COMMUNITY OVERLAY rather than a shipped catalogue, so it is already covered by the \"unvouched by construction\" half of the INV-nular disclosure and is lower priority: elixir `Req.new` under net_send (Req.new/1 builds a %Req.Request{} struct; Req.get!/post! send). Named here so the family is complete, not to schedule it.\n\nand a set of correct ones that must NOT move: fileinput.FileInput, gzip.GzipFile, tarfile.TarFile, zipfile.ZipFile, io.FileIO, tempfile.*, ctypes.cdll.LoadLibrary -- all open a file AT CONSTRUCTION. `ets.new` is the closest sibling to the F1 mechanism this item's parent already fixed (in-process cells declared db_*), differing only in that ETS is a shared table rather than a per-process ref, which is a vocabulary question and not an obvious deletion.\n\nWHAT IS ASKED. Adjudicate the four candidates against source, then A/B whatever moves on a cohort exercising urllib and net/http. The pre-registered question is not \"does the row go away\" but \"does the flow survive the row going away\" -- if `secret -> Request(data=secret) -> urlopen(req)` still reports after the deletion, this is a free precision fix; if it does not, the row is holding up real findings and the correct fix is propagation through the constructor, not deletion. State which before running.\n\nFILED FROM T1 (the INV-nular re-scope, 2026-09-08). INV-nular closes on its re-scoped statement, which names this row explicitly under \"what this does not assert\" -- close on the filed row, file the residual."  # 6d7c
    fields:  # 6d7c
      statement: "A CONSTRUCTOR is catalogued under a data-transfer boundary, so a call that builds an object and performs no I/O is credited with the crossing. Live at dev f4f278b736 (2026-09-08), verified by listing the loaded rows rather than by predicate:\n\n    python   net_send  urllib.request.Request     BASE, no notes\n    go       net_send  net/http.NewRequest        BASE, no notes\n    erlang   db_write  ets.new                    BASE, no notes\n    elixir   db_write  ets.new                    BASE, no notes\n    elixir   net_send  Req.new                    COMMUNITY OVERLAY (already covered by the unvouched disclosure)\n\n`Request` and `NewRequest` build a request object and send nothing; the send is the separate `urlopen` / `Client.Do` row. `ets.new` creates a table and stores nothing; `ets.insert` is the write and is separately rowed, so one logical write is credited twice.\n\nCORRECT ROWS THAT MUST NOT MOVE, listed so a fix cannot over-delete: fileinput.FileInput, gzip.GzipFile, tarfile.TarFile, zipfile.ZipFile, io.FileIO, tempfile.* and ctypes.cdll.LoadLibrary all open a file AT CONSTRUCTION and are rightly rowed; objc NSData.initWithContentsOfFile: and scala Source.fromFile are the same shape one language over."  # 6d7c
      root_cause: "INV-nular's root cause (a row binds a name to a boundary with no check that the primitive performs it) in the specific shape the existing guards cannot see. The direction sweep (test_inv_nular_false_sources.py::test_the_direction_sweep_is_otherwise_clean) is NAME-BASED and examines only names carrying a direction word from a curated vocabulary -- 598 of 3,405 loaded primitive records at tip, 18%. \"Request\", \"NewRequest\" and \"new\" state no direction, so no sweep ever examined them; the syntactic constructor stratum of the 2026-08-28 census over-collects so heavily in Go (146 of 181 candidates, because every exported Go identifier is capitalised) that reading it by hand did not surface them either.\n\nSECOND-ORDER CAUSE: being ON the path of a crossing is treated as performing it. The catalogue states this defect in its own notes elsewhere (\"part of the read path\"), and F3 (query builders) is the same mistake already fixed once."  # 6d7c
